    phase "design-system" state="complete" worked-by="agent:principal-engineer" {
        approach "a manifest is PATCHED in place, never re-rendered whole" {
            over "composing each manifest from nothing, the way a released document is"
            over "one canonical template all six harnesses render from"
            because "a manifest is only PARTLY derived — version and description come from \
                the record, and skills paths, hooks and per-harness structure are facts about \
                how doctrine reaches that harness, not facts this engine has an opinion about. \
                Re-composing the whole file would mean deciding a shape that was never wrong; \
                what was wrong was three facts inside a shape that already was right"
        }

        approach "drift is judged field by field, on the parsed value, not by comparing bytes" {
            over "comparing the whole re-rendered document to what is on disk, byte for byte, \
                the way a released document is verified"
            over "hashing only the fields this engine writes"
            because "the first build did exactly the byte comparison an archival document gets, \
                and it flagged every hand-authored manifest as drifted from itself — reformatting \
                a file this engine only partly owns is not the fact `hand-edited` is about, and a \
                rule that cannot tell the two apart is a rule nobody can trust the day it fires"
        }

        approach "a manifest's text is fed to the SAME retired-vocabulary rule a skill is, \
            never a manifest-specific copy of it" {
            over "a manifest-specific rule for retired words, mirroring `surface-teaches-a-\
                retired-kind`"
            because "a manifest is shipped text an adopter reads before any skill exists for \
                them, and `surface-teaches-a-retired-kind` already asks exactly one question of \
                any shipped text: does it instruct in a word the method retired. Two rules \
                asking the same question is the second-copy problem this frame keeps finding \
                inside itself"
        }

        approach "the package's own `files` list is asked directly whether it ships `crates` \
            and `praxis`, rather than modelled as a manifest with derived fields" {
            over "treating the npm package's own manifest as a sixth derived manifest"
            because "the npm package's manifest carries no version or description this record derives — its \
                fault is narrower and different in kind: every skill instructs an agent to run a \
                command, and the file list is either honest about containing that command or it \
                is not. A yes/no question does not need the projection machinery the five \
                manifests do"
        }

        produced "four approaches, one new dependency, a `manifest` module, two new rules and \
            their witnesses, and the six real files corrected"
